Myth: When the world was young, the sky lay low and heavy, pressing hills flat and folding birds against the reeds. No one had seen a shadow, for there was no room for light to stand beside anything. In the marsh lived Luma, a potter with clay beneath her nails and a moth’s patience in her heart.

At night she heard cranes dreaming of height and children dreaming of running upright. She dug the deepest mud, mixed it with dew, ash, and a fallen star-seed, then shaped a wide bowl. Into its rim she carved every face she loved, so the sky would have company.

Luma climbed the oldest willow until its top brushed blue darkness. “Sky,” she called, “lift yourself, and I will give you this mirror of the earth.”

The sky trembled. “If I rise, I will be alone.”

“Look into the bowl,” said Luma. “We will shine there.”

So the sky rose, carrying the bowl, and the bowl became the moon. Its pale light cast the first shadows, proving that things had space around them. Birds opened their wings. Trees stood tall. People laughed.

Still the moon watches over the marsh, filled with Luma’s promise: distance need not mean loneliness.
